result:
### Rationale
E3 answers H12, and the answer is no: 68% is not a TF32-only hardware ceiling. Under harness conditions (dirty-L2 flush, CUPTI, clock steady at 1963–1975 MHz, read inside the kernel), cuBLAS gets 70–75% of the clock peak on square TF32 GEMMs and 69–79% on BF16, three rounds, spread under 3%. Our shape lands in the same 73–75% range once N padding is counted. When the card is power-capped to 1.2–1.33 GHz under sustained load, the same kernels reach 89% (TF32) and 94–96% (BF16) per clock. So the limit does not scale with the SM clock. That rules out an SM-side shared-memory or TF32 issue-rate cap. Cutting L2 traffic by multicasting W in a 2×2 cluster made it 20–30% slower, and the K-tile and swizzle sweep found nothing better than r1. The code below is r1 unchanged apart from comments. The −7% at L in run_tests is the rented card's clock state drifting, not a real gain.

